RAWM.CONF(5)

# NAME

rawm.conf - runtime configuration for rawm

# DESCRIPTION

*rawm*(1) uses libconfig to load window-manager policy at startup.
Configuration changes therefore do not require recompiling rawm.

The configuration is deliberately split into groups for user-interface
settings, behavior, layouts, tags, application rules, commands, and input
bindings. Unknown setting names, wrong types, invalid ranges, unresolved
commands or value references, invalid key symbols, duplicate bindings, and
references to tags or layouts which do not exist are fatal configuration
errors.

Use *rawm -C* to validate the effective configuration without opening the X
display.

# FILES AND PRECEDENCE

Without *-c*, rawm starts from its compiled-in conservative defaults and then
loads these files, if present, in order:

_/etc/rawm/rawm.conf_
	System configuration. The directory is controlled by Meson's *sysconfdir*
	build option.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawm.conf_
	Per-user configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is an absolute path.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawm.conf_
	Per-user fallback when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is unset, empty, or relative.

A later layer overrides an earlier layer. *rawm -c path* bypasses both standard
files and loads only the built-in defaults followed by _path_.

Scalar values and ordinary group members overlay individually. The named
members of *commands* merge by command name. Color schemes and layout symbols
also overlay member by member.

When present, *tags.default*, *tags.monitors*, *rules*, *input.keys*, and
*input.buttons* replace the corresponding complete value inherited from the
previous layer. This makes list policy deterministic: a user does not
accidentally retain an old rule or binding merely because it was omitted from
a replacement list.

# LIBCONFIG SYNTAX

The file uses normal libconfig syntax. Groups use braces, lists use
parentheses, strings are quoted, and statements end in semicolons. For example:

```
ui = {
  font = "monospace:size=9";
  border_width = 2;
};
```

libconfig includes are supported. rawm sets the include directory to the
directory containing the file being loaded, so a relative include is relative
to that configuration file:

```
@include "workstation.conf"
```

# VALUE REFERENCES

rawm extends ordinary libconfig strings with *${path}* references. References
are resolved only after all configuration layers have been merged, so an
inherited command observes the final user override of a referenced setting.
This is semantic expansion of the typed rawm configuration, not textual
preprocessing of the configuration file.

References may appear in *ui.font*, color values, layout symbols, and command
argument strings. Literal text may surround a reference:

```
ui = {
  font = "IBM Plex Mono:style=Regular:size=11";
  opacity = 0.85;

  colors = {
    normal = { background = "#282828"; };
    selected = { background = "${ui.colors.normal.background}"; };
  };
};

commands = {
  launcher = (
    "dmenu_run",
    "-fn", "${ui.font}",
    "-nb", "${ui.colors.normal.background}",
    "-sb", "${ui.colors.selected.background}"
  );
  transset = ("transset", "-a", "-t", "${ui.opacity}");
};
```

The reference namespace contains these scalar paths:

- *ui.font*, *ui.border_width*, *ui.snap*, *ui.opacity*
- *ui.bar.show*, *ui.bar.position*, *ui.bar.height*, *ui.bar.padding*,
  *ui.bar.item_spacing*, *ui.bar.section_spacing*, *ui.bar.cell_margin*,
  *ui.bar.indicator_size*,
  *ui.bar.tags.visibility*
- *ui.osd.show*, *ui.osd.position*, *ui.osd.duration_ms*,
  *ui.osd.padding*, *ui.osd.offset*, *ui.osd.min_width*
- *ui.systray.show*, *ui.systray.spacing*
- *status.enabled*, *status.protocol*, *status.separator*
- *ui.colors.<scheme>.<component>*, where _scheme_ is *normal*, *selected*,
  *urgent*, *error*, or *floating*, and _component_ is *border*,
  *foreground*, or *background*
- *behavior.dialog_center*, *behavior.size_hints*
- *layout.master_factor*, *layout.master_count*
- *layout.gaps.inner*, *layout.gaps.outer*, *layout.gaps.smart*
- *layout.symbols.<layout>*, where _layout_ is one of the stable layout names
  documented below

String targets are expanded recursively. Numeric scalars are rendered in
base-10 form, booleans as *true* or *false*, *ui.bar.position* as *top* or
*bottom*, and *status.protocol* as its protocol name. *status.separator* resolves to its final string value. Groups, lists, rules, tags, commands, and individual command
arguments are not reference targets.

An unknown path, malformed reference, or reference cycle is a fatal
configuration error and is checked by *rawm -C*. rawm does not interpret
references as environment variables and does not perform shell expansion. To
pass a literal shell-style parameter through rawm, prefix the dollar sign with
another dollar sign: *$${HOME}* becomes literal *${HOME}*. This is useful in an
explicit *sh -c* command.

# UI

The *ui* group controls presentation and pointer geometry.

*font* = string
	Fontconfig/Xft font pattern. The default is *monospace:size=9*.

*border_width* = integer
	Window border width in pixels. Range 0..64. Default 2.

*snap* = integer
	Distance in pixels at which floating windows snap to monitor edges. Range
	0..4096. Default 32.

*opacity* = number
	Opacity applied to rawm-created surfaces where supported. Range 0.0..1.0.
	The conservative default is 1.0.

## Bar

The *ui.bar* group accepts:

*show* = boolean
	Whether the bar starts visible. Default true.

*position* = "top" | "bottom"
	Bar edge. Default "bottom".

*height* = integer
	Explicit bar height in pixels, 0..512. Zero derives the height from the
	font. Default 0.

*padding* = integer
	Horizontal inset at each outer edge of the bar, in pixels. Range 0..128.
	Default 0.

*item_spacing* = integer
	Horizontal spacing between adjacent tag labels and between adjacent status
	segments, in pixels. Range 0..128. Default 0.

*section_spacing* = integer
	Horizontal separation between the tag, layout, title, status, and tray
	sections, in pixels. Range 0..256. Default 0.

*cell_margin* = integer
	Vertical margin in pixels around semantic bar cells such as viewed tags,
	layout mode cells, and warning/critical status cells. Zero keeps the
	legacy full-height cell rendering. Range 0..64. Default 0.

*indicator_size* = integer
	Thickness in pixels of the compact focused-title authority marker drawn
	along the workspace-facing edge of the title region. Zero disables the
	marker. Range 0..32. Default 0.

The nested *ui.bar.tags* group accepts:

*visibility* = "occupied" | "all"
	Controls which configured tags have labels in the bar. *"occupied"* shows
	tags containing normal clients plus every currently viewed tag; an all-tags
	client does not by itself make every tag appear occupied. *"all"* always
	shows every configured tag. Default *"occupied"*.

Tag labels communicate state through the configured color schemes rather than
through dwm-style occupancy squares. Viewed and urgent tags use the configured
selected or urgent foreground/background as inset semantic cells, with their
border color repeated as a workspace-facing authority marker. Other visible
tags use *ui.colors.normal*. Viewed state takes precedence over urgency.

The layout label is rendered as an inset neutral mode cell. The selected window title
is centered in the title region and remains on the normal bar background.
When *indicator_size* is non-zero, a compact marker centered on the
workspace-facing edge uses the selected or floating border color to describe
the focused client's authority state. Warning and critical status segments use their semantic foreground/background
as inset cells and repeat their semantic border color as a workspace-facing
marker.

Drawing and mouse hit testing consume the same visibility and spacing policy,
so suppressed tags and inter-item gaps reserve no clickable tag space.

## OSD

The *ui.osd* group controls rawm's transient keyboard-feedback surface.

*show* = boolean
	Enable the OSD. Default false.

*position* = "top" | "bottom"
	Place the OSD near the corresponding workspace edge. The bar is excluded
	from workspace geometry, so a bottom OSD naturally sits above a bottom
	bar. Default "bottom".

*duration_ms* = integer
	Time the OSD remains visible after the most recent update. Range
	100..5000. Default 800.

*padding* = integer
	Internal horizontal and vertical padding in pixels. Range 0..64.
	Default 10.

*offset* = integer
	Distance from the selected monitor's workspace edge in pixels. Range
	0..512. Default 24.

*min_width* = integer
	Minimum OSD width in pixels. Text wider than this value still expands the
	surface as needed. Zero preserves content-sized geometry. Range 0..4096.
	Default 0.

The OSD is a rawm-owned override-redirect surface with
*\_RAWM\_SURFACE = "osd"* and *\_RAWM\_MONITOR* metadata. It does not take focus,
reserve work area, or enter the client list. Layout, view, master-area,
floating, and fullscreen actions update the same surface and reset its
timeout.

## System tray

The *ui.systray* group accepts:

*show* = boolean
	Whether the system tray is shown when rawm was built with
	*-Dsystray=true*. Default true.

*spacing* = integer
	Spacing around tray icons in pixels. Range 0..64. Default 2.

## Colors

The *ui.colors* group has five named schemes: *normal*, *selected*, *urgent*,
*error*, and *floating*. Each scheme may contain *border*, *foreground*, and
*background* string values accepted by Xft. The *floating* scheme is used for
the selected client when rawm places that client on the elevated/floating
plane, including the explicit floating layout. If omitted it defaults to the
*selected* scheme's built-in colors, preserving the historical appearance.

```
ui = {
  colors = {
    normal = {
      border = "#cccccc";
      foreground = "#000000";
      background = "#cccccc";
    };
  };
};
```

# STATUS

The top-level *status* group defines the external status boundary consumed by
rawm. It does not name or supervise a producer. A session may use
*rawmstat*(1) or any other X11 client that implements the selected protocol.

*enabled* = boolean
	Whether rawm consumes and displays external status segments. When false,
	the status area is empty and status-property changes are ignored. Default
	true.

*protocol* = "rawm-v2"
	Status transport and payload contract. *"rawm-v2"* is the only supported
	protocol and is the default. Unknown protocol names are fatal configuration
	errors.

*separator* = string
	Printable UTF-8 rendered between adjacent status segments. The separator
	is presentation policy owned by rawm, not by the producer. The built-in
	default is *" | "*. An empty string is allowed. The value may contain at
	most 256 bytes and may not contain ASCII control characters.

## rawm-v2

A producer writes the *\_RAWM\_STATUS\_V2* property of the X root window.
The property type must be *UTF8_STRING* with format 8 and its payload may
contain at most 4096 bytes. The payload is zero or more records in this exact
textual form:

```
id<TAB>state<TAB>text<LF>
```

The final record also ends in *LF*. The fields have these contracts:

- _id_ is 1..64 ASCII characters from *A-Z*, *a-z*, *0-9*, period, underscore,
  or hyphen. Segment identifiers must be unique within one property value.
- _state_ is exactly *normal*, *warning*, or *critical*.
- _text_ is non-empty printable UTF-8. ASCII control characters, including
  tab and newline, are not permitted in text.

The property name carries the protocol version, so there is no in-band version
header and no escaping language. Any malformed record, duplicate identifier,
wrong property type, invalid UTF-8, embedded NUL, missing final newline, or
oversized payload invalidates the complete property and rawm displays an empty
status area.

Segment state is semantic rather than presentational. rawm maps states to its
existing palette:

- *normal* -> *ui.colors.normal*
- *warning* -> *ui.colors.urgent*
- *critical* -> *ui.colors.error*

The configured *status.separator* is rendered with *ui.colors.normal*. Segment
identifiers are not displayed; they provide stable identity for protocol users
and future interaction such as per-segment click routing.

The root *WM_NAME* property is not used as a status channel. *rawm-v2* carries
no color values, markup, process discovery, or producer lifecycle.

# BEHAVIOR

The *behavior* group accepts:

*dialog_center* = "none" | "monitor" | "parent"
	Placement policy for EWMH dialog windows. Dialogs remain floating in all
	modes. *"none"* leaves their position unchanged. *"monitor"* centers the
	dialog's outer frame in its monitor. *"parent"* centers it over the outer
	frame of its managed *WM_TRANSIENT_FOR* owner on the same monitor; when no
	usable managed owner exists, rawm falls back to monitor centering. Default
	*"none"*.

*size_hints* = "never" | "floating" | "always"
	Controls when rawm applies ICCCM *WM_NORMAL_HINTS* while it owns a resize.
	*"never"* ignores application size hints, *"floating"* applies them only
	when geometry is free (a floating client or the floating layout), and
	*"always"* also applies them to arranged layouts. Default *"floating"*.

*floating_placement* = "client" | "cascade"
	Initial placement policy for newly managed normal clients whose geometry is
	free. *"client"* preserves the admitted client coordinates. *"cascade"*
	places otherwise-unpositioned clients at deterministic offsets from the
	selected visible free client on the destination monitor. ICCCM *USPosition*
	is treated as user authority and bypasses the cascade; *PPosition* is only a
	program preference and may be replaced. Dialogs, transients, and explicit
	*center* rules keep their existing placement authorities. Default
	*"cascade"*.

*cascade_step* = integer
	Pixel inset/offset used by *floating_placement = "cascade"*. Range 1..4096.
	The first cascade slot is inset from the monitor work-area origin by this
	amount. Later clients are offset from the selected visible free client; a
	placement that would cross the right or bottom work-area edge wraps to the
	first slot. Default 32.

# LAYOUT

The *layout* group accepts:

*master_factor* = number
	Initial fraction of monitor width/height assigned to the master area.
	Range 0.05..0.95. Default 0.55.

*master_count* = integer
	Initial number of clients in the master area. Range 0..64. Default 1.

*gaps* = group
	Controls spacing around and between tiled clients. Floating clients are not
	affected.

	*inner* = integer
		Pixels between tiled clients. Range 0..4096. Default 0.

	*outer* = integer
		Pixels between the monitor work area and tiled clients. Range 0..4096.
		Default 0.

	*smart* = boolean
		When true, suppress the outer gap while exactly one tiled client is
		visible on the monitor. Inner gaps are unaffected. Default false.

*symbols* = group
	Overrides status-bar symbols by stable layout name. The configured symbol is
	the authoritative prefix used by dynamic bar labels; rawm appends the master
	count or current/total client count where that layout reports one.

The stable layout names are *tile*, *floating*, *monocle*, *bstack*,
*bstack_horizontal*, and *grid*.

```
layout = {
  gaps = {
    inner = 8;
    outer = 8;
    smart = true;
  };

  symbols = {
    tile = "[]=";
    floating = "<1/1>";
    monocle = "[1/1]";
    bstack = "TTT";
    bstack_horizontal = "===";
    grid = "###";
  };
};
```

Gaps apply to *tile*, *monocle*, *bstack*, *bstack_horizontal*, and *grid*.
For monocle there are no inner gaps because clients overlap; only the outer
gap applies. rawm clamps configured gaps to the available monitor geometry so
an excessively large value cannot produce negative layout dimensions.

Configuration refers to layouts by these names, never by implementation array
indexes.

Arranged layouts, initial free-window placement, and per-client saved free
geometry are separate authorities. When rawm first manages a normal client
whose geometry is free, *behavior.floating_placement* may choose its initial
position before that rectangle is saved. Explicit centering, dialog/transient
placement, and user-authoritative *USPosition* take precedence over the default
cascade. Tile, monocle, bstack, bstack_horizontal, and grid may then replace the
live rectangle without overwriting the saved free rectangle.

Switching the monitor to the *floating* layout releases the current live
rectangles in place. It does not rerun initial placement, restore historical
per-client geometry, or move/resize clients merely because the arranger was
removed. Moves, resizes, and accepted client ConfigureRequest geometry while
the monitor is in the floating layout update the client's saved free geometry.

Toggling an individual client floating is a different operation: that explicit
client transition restores the client's saved free geometry. Fullscreen is
temporary state and does not replace the saved free rectangle.

The *behavior.size_hints* policy is applied when rawm materializes a rectangle.
With the default *"floating"* policy, a terminal's resize increments or another
client's min/max/aspect hints may therefore adjust an explicit per-client
floating restore or a later free resize, while changing the monitor layout to
*floating* preserves the existing rectangle exactly. Use *"never"* to ignore
those hints for rawm-owned free resizing, or *"always"* to apply hints to
arranged layouts as well.

# TAGS

The *tags.default* setting is a list of 1..31 tag groups. Each entry requires a
*name* and may select a default *layout*. Tag names may contain at most 22
bytes.

```
tags = {
  default = (
    { name = "term"; layout = "tile"; },
    { name = "web";  layout = "monocle"; },
    { name = "chat"; layout = "tile"; }
  );
};
```

The list length is the effective tag count. Input bindings and rules may not
refer to a tag beyond that count.

The optional *tags.monitors* list overrides tag names and default layouts for
specific monitor numbers. Every override must define exactly the same number
of tags as *tags.default*.

```
tags = {
  monitors = (
    {
      monitor = 1;
      tags = (
        { name = "1:dev"; layout = "tile"; },
        { name = "2:web"; layout = "monocle"; },
        { name = "3";     layout = "tile"; }
      );
    }
  );
};
```

Monitor numbers are zero-based.

# RULES

*rules* is a list of groups matched when a client is managed. An omitted match
field is a wildcard. String match fields use substring matching, preserving
rawm's existing rule semantics.

A rule accepts:

*class* = string
	WM_CLASS class substring.

*instance* = string
	WM_CLASS instance substring.

*title* = string
	Window-title substring.

*role* = string
	WM_WINDOW_ROLE substring.

*tags* = list | "all"
	One-based tag numbers, for example *(1, 3)*, or the string *"all"*. If
	omitted or empty, the client remains on the currently selected tags.

*floating* = boolean
	Start the matching client floating.

*center* = "none" | "monitor" | "parent"
	Explicit centering policy for the matching client. *"none"* disables
	centering, *"monitor"* centers the client's outer frame in its monitor, and
	*"parent"* centers it over its managed *WM_TRANSIENT_FOR* owner on the same
	monitor, with monitor centering as the fallback. For EWMH dialogs, an explicit rule value takes
	precedence over *behavior.dialog_center*. If omitted, non-dialog clients are
	not centered and dialogs use *behavior.dialog_center*.

*fullscreen* = boolean
	Set the matching client's initial fullscreen state. *true* starts the client
	fullscreen on its assigned monitor after monitor, tags, floating state, and
	other rule placement have been resolved. *false* explicitly suppresses an
	application-requested initial fullscreen state. If omitted, the client's
	initial *\_NET\_WM\_STATE\_FULLSCREEN* request is honored. This is an initial
	placement policy only; later EWMH fullscreen requests and the
	*toggle_fullscreen* action remain effective.

*attention* = "none" | "urgent"
	Initial attention policy for the matching client. *"urgent"* latches
	rawm-owned attention only when the client's resolved tags are not visible on
	its destination monitor. The hidden tag is then rendered with
	*ui.colors.urgent* until the client is focused. *"none"* explicitly cancels
	an attention policy established by an earlier matching rule. If omitted, the
	rule does not modify attention policy.

	Rule-owned attention is separate from the application's ICCCM
	*XUrgencyHint*. rawm does not manufacture or overwrite that client-owned hint
	when a rule requests attention. Application urgency and rawm-owned attention
	are combined only for presentation and are both consumed when the client is
	focused. This is an initial notification policy, not continuous enforcement.

*monitor* = integer
	Zero-based destination monitor. *-1* means the current monitor and is the
	default.

Example:

```
rules = (
  {
    class = "Pavucontrol";
    floating = true;
    center = "monitor";
    monitor = -1;
  },
  {
    class = "mpv";
    tags = (8);
    fullscreen = true;
    attention = "urgent";
  }
);
```

Rules are evaluated for transient clients as well as ordinary top-level
clients. A transient first inherits its managed owner's monitor and tags, then
matching rules may refine that placement policy. This allows per-application
dialog rules to override the global dialog-centering mode.

When several rules match, an explicitly present *attention* value overrides an
earlier matching rule's attention policy. A client placed on a tag that is
already visible on its destination monitor does not acquire rule-owned urgency.

An explicitly present *rules* list replaces the inherited rule list.

# COMMANDS

*commands* is a group whose member names are command identifiers and whose
values are non-empty argv lists.

```
commands = {
  terminal = ("st");
  launcher = ("dmenu_run", "-p", "Run:");
  shell_example = ("/bin/sh", "-c", "printf '%s\\n' hello");
};
```

Commands are executed directly with *execvp*(3). rawm does not implicitly
invoke a shell. Use an explicit shell argv when shell parsing is actually
wanted.

Command definitions merge by name across configuration layers. A later
*terminal* definition replaces only the earlier *terminal* definition. Command
arguments may use the *${path}* value references described above; expansion
happens after all layers have been merged.

# INPUT

The *input* group contains optional *keys* and *buttons* lists. If either list
is present, that entire list replaces the inherited list of the same kind.

## Modifiers

A binding's *modifiers* member is a list containing zero or more of *"Shift"*,
*"Control"*, *"Mod1"*, *"Mod2"*, *"Mod3"*, *"Mod4"*, and *"Mod5"*.

An empty list means no modifier.

## Key bindings

Each *input.keys* entry requires:

*modifiers* = list
	Optional modifier list. Omission is equivalent to an empty list.

*key* = string
	An X keysym name understood by *XStringToKeysym*(3), such as *"Return"*,
	*"space"*, *"1"*, or *"XF86AudioMute"*.

*action* = string
	Action name described below.

Action-specific members are supplied in the same group.

```
input.keys = (
  {
    modifiers = ("Mod4", "Shift");
    key = "Return";
    action = "spawn";
    command = "terminal";
  },
  {
    modifiers = ("Mod4");
    key = "2";
    action = "view";
    tag = 2;
  }
);
```

Duplicate resolved modifier/keysym pairs are rejected.

## Mouse bindings

Each *input.buttons* entry requires *click*, *button*, and *action*. The
optional *modifiers* list has the same syntax as key bindings.

*click* may be *"tag_bar"*, *"layout"*, *"status"*, *"title"*, *"client"*,
or *"root"*. The *"title"* area requires a build with
*-Dwindow_title=true*.

*button* is an integer from 1 through 9.

For *click = "status"*, optional *status_id* targets one *rawm-v2* segment by
its stable identifier. The identifier uses the same 1..64 character syntax as
the status protocol. Hit testing uses the same segment widths, separator, and
item spacing as rendering. A binding without *status_id* is a fallback for the
whole status area, including separator/gap space. Segment-specific bindings
take precedence over the fallback when both match the same button and
modifiers. *status_id* is invalid for other click areas.

For *view*, *toggle_view*, *tag*, and *toggle_tag* on *"tag_bar"*, omitting the
*tag* member means the tag under the pointer. This is how the conventional tag
bar bindings are expressed.

```
input.buttons = (
  {
    click = "client";
    modifiers = ("Mod4");
    button = 1;
    action = "move";
  },
  {
    click = "tag_bar";
    button = 1;
    action = "view";
  }
);
```

# ACTIONS

*spawn*
	Requires *command* naming an entry in *commands*.

*toggle_bar*, *rename_tag*, *zoom*, *kill_client*, *toggle_floating*,
*window_view*, *toggle_fullscreen*, *move*, *resize*
	Take no action-specific argument.

*focus_stack*
	Requires non-zero integer *number*. Positive selects forward, negative
	selects backward.

*focus_index*
	Requires positive integer *number*.

*inc_master*
	Requires integer *number*, usually 1 or -1.

*set_master_factor*
	Requires either absolute *factor* in the range 0.1..0.9 or relative
	*delta* strictly between -0.9 and 0.9. The two members are mutually
	exclusive.

*view*
	Optional *tag* is a one-based tag number or *"all"*. With no tag in a
	key binding, switches to the previous view. With no tag on a tag-bar
	mouse binding, uses the clicked tag.

*toggle_view*
	Requires *tag* except on a tag-bar mouse binding, where omission uses the
	clicked tag.

*tag*, *toggle_tag*
	Require *tag* except on a tag-bar mouse binding, where omission uses the
	clicked tag. *tag* may be a one-based number or *"all"*.

*set_layout*
	Optional *layout* is one of the stable layout names. If omitted, toggles
	between the current and previous layout.

*focus_monitor*, *tag_monitor*
	Require non-zero integer *number*. Negative selects the previous monitor;
	positive selects the next monitor.

*quit*
	Optional boolean *restart*. False exits rawm; true re-executes it with the
	original command-line arguments.

Arguments not used by an action are rejected rather than silently ignored.

# VALIDATION

The recommended edit cycle is:

```
rawm -C
```

To validate a file independently of the standard system and user layers:

```
rawm -C -c ./rawm.conf
```

Successful validation exits with status 0 and does not require an X display.
Configuration errors are printed to standard error and produce a non-zero exit
status.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm*(1), *libconfig*(5), *XStringToKeysym*(3), *execvp*(3)

